#*******************************************************************************
# This site is hosted on Cloudflare Workers. It doesn't use .htaccess. In this
# file, I describe
#   	2. Cloudflare configuration & rules (I use now)
#*******************************************************************************

# * REDIRECT HTTP & NON-WWW TO WWW:
#
# It's important to note;
#	1. Cloudflare has an option to require https (SSL > Edge Certificates >
#     Always use...). I don't use that because http://mcacc-collars.charity
#     would be redirected twice. First, by that option (to https), and then to
#     www by the following rules.
#    
#     Note: I do use the http header "Strict-Transport-Security:" to require
#     https. See docs/_headers
#   2. I don't redirect if the path starts with /.well-known/
#     RFC 8615 has some requirements for never redirecting to www if it's not
#     specified. And, not to https in some cases. I choose not to do anything
#     with it. (But, .php requests are blocked below.).
#
#    http & non-www redirects can be done (as one redirect) in two places:
#
#   1. "Bulk redirects:"
#     - Go to home (domains) > "Delivery & performance > Bulk redirects."
# 	  - Create a "bulk redirect list" with these three items:
#	 
#     SOURCE URL                        TARGET URL                         STATUS
#     http://mcacc-collars.charity/     https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/ 301
#     https://mcacc-collars.charity/    "                                  301
#     http://www.mcacc-collars.charity/ "                                  301
#
#     You MUST enable these "parameters" for EACH rule:
#       - Subpath matching (matches any source URL after /) 
#       - Preserve path suffix (adds anything after / to the target URL)
#      
#       - I also enable "preserve query string" because my links to .css & .js
#       use query strings for cache busting. (Any other requests with a query
#       string are blocked in Security > Security Rules.).
#
#   Those rules cover every possible condition with just one redirect.
#
#   Next, create a "Bulk Redirect Rule," and choose the option "use expression
#   editor." Enter this expression in the editor (not the "builder"):
#
#     http.request.full_uri in $apex_to_www and
#     (not starts_with(http.request.uri.path, "/.well-known/"))
#
#    That rule will limit the redirects to everything except /.well-known/
#
#    I don't understand the other options there ("apply matching url" Name &
#    "key".) Mine are set to name = "apex_to_www", and key = "http.request.full_uri".
#    (I've never been able to use a different key value.).
#
# 2. Instead, you can do those redirects in "Rules > Overview > Redirect rules"
#    as a "custom filter expression:
#
#   - "When incoming requests match..."
#     - Chose "Edit expression:"
#       not starts_with(http.request.uri.path, "/.well-known/") and
#          (
#          not ssl or 
#          http.host eq "mcacc-collars.charity"
#          )
#	  
#   - "Then..."
#     - "URL redirect"
#       Type: Dynamic
#       Expression: concat("https://www.mcacc-collars.charity", http.request.uri.path)
#       Status: 301
#       Preserve query string (checked. Query strings are used with .css & .js links)
#
# #1 & 2 each have their pros & cons:
#
# The 1st way (Bulk): applied after the blocking rules. This way seems more 
# efficient to knock out malicious requests out (first, instead of redirecting
# them, and then blocking them). Security > Analytics will have less noise
# (explained next). 
# 
# The downside: normal traffic that needs to be redirected will go through the
# whitelisting rules (below) twice. It's probably better to let the
# inefficiency of the 2nd way (Rules) affect the malicious traffic than to let
# inefficiency apply to the normal traffic. But, there's not that much normal
# traffic compared to the flood of malicious traffic I get.
#
# The 2nd way (Rules) are applied early in Cloudflare's process.[1] The upside
# is that normal traffic is redirected quickly (only goes through the whitelist
# rules just once, after the redirect).

# The downside: malicious traffic gets redirected and then blocked when it
# returns. That seems inefficient. But, I guess I shouldn't care (let the
# malicious people waste their time with redirects.).
#
# The real downside: Those redirects (malicious) appear in Security > Analytics,
# making that page less useable to see real traffic. You can omit the display of
# blocked requests (below). But, you can't omit those requests when they're
# redirected (before they're blocked). The analytics screen gets junky.
#
# I think Rules (#2) is how it should be done. If I didn't look at the analytics
# I would use Rules.
#
#
# * REDIRECT other things
#
#   /images/[subdirectory] to /images/ 
#
# I created another bulk-redirect list for the /images/medium/ & /small/
# subdirectories. They have smaller versions of the images in /images. I have
# directory browsing for /images, but not for those subdirectories. I redirect
# any request to browse those subdirs to /images/:
#
#
# SOURCE URL                     								TARGET URL									STATUS
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/medium				https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/ 	301
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/medium/				"											301
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/medium/index.html	"											301
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/small				"											301
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/small/				"											301
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/small/index.html		"											301
#
# Do not enable any "parameters."
#
# I also bulk-redirect 3 other things:
#
#   "/license.txt" to "/LICENSE.txt"
#   "/manifest.webmanifest" to "/site.webmanifest"
#   "/security.txt" to "/.well-known/security.txt"
#
# Those are valid requests, but served by the other names. I fix that with those.
#
# * REDIRECT (short-lived docs/_redirects)
#
# I consider the above (bulk) redirects to be forever. When I have to redirect
# a filename that's changed, I use the docs/_redirects file. I consider these
# short-lived (even though they're 301 permanent). I.e., after a few months
# there shouldn't be anymore requests; the redirect will be removed. (At that
# time, I create a "workers routes" in Cloudflare's UI to return 410 (Gone)
# because crawlers keep checking until they see it really gone. That's done with
# workers/return-410.js. See that file for more info.).
#
#
#===
# * BLOCK MALICIOUS REQUESTS
#
# I was getting hundreds of requests for system files, things I don't have
# (nobody should try to access even if I did). I created the following rules in
# "Security > Security rules > Custom rules:" 
#
# RULE #1: Whitelist: expected (valid) requests
#
# Since this site is so static (limited number of urls), it seemed better to
# whitelist what I know to be valid requests first. This lets me be more brought
# blocking everything else. I start with what should be the most frequent
# (images?), end with the least expected (someone typing in a path without a
# trailing /).
#
# The first thing I do is expect anything (I'm going to whitelist below) to have
# proper method, and no query string (unless it's .css or .js. I use a query-
# string to bust the cache.):

(
http.request.method in {"GET" "HEAD" "OPTIONS"} and

(http.request.uri.query eq "" or
http.request.uri.path in {"/css/style.css" "/css/files.css" "/js/darkmode.js"}) 

) 

# This creates a bunch of or's evaluated within the and-condition. There is a
# close-parenthisis at the very end.

and (

# If a request isn't "POST" (etc, and has no query string), then I think
# /images/* gets the most access. I whitelist it first"

(starts_with(http.request.uri.path, "/images/") and
  substring(http.request.uri.path, -4) in {"webp" ".jpg" ".gif" ".svg"} and
  len(http.request.uri.path) gt 28
) or

# I expect only four extensions (.webp, .jpg, .gif or .svg; plus .html which is
# whitelisted further down because it should be rarely specified in the path).
# The shortest image filename is 21-char (plus 8 for "/images/" path).
#
# There can also be directory browsing on /images/ (not the subdirs inside it.
# Attempts to do that are redirected to /images/). That should be much less
# frequent. I whitelist it below. The .html extension is handled there too.
#
# Next, I assume /css & /js are the next most-common requests:

http.request.uri.path in {"/css/style.css" "/css/files.css" "/js/darkmode.js"} or

# Again, there could be directory browsing on those. That's whitelisted below
# because it should be rare. (I don't have directory browsing enabled for those.
# But, it shouldn't be blocked either.).
#
# I think the published (displayable) pages (directories) of the site would be
# next most commonly requested (in the order I think they'd be most visited, not
# sure if order matters with the "in" operator?):

http.request.uri.path in {
"/resources/leash-sleeve/"
"/resources/leash-sleeve/template/"
"/resources/collars-leashes/"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn/"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn/bobbin-winder/"
"/resources/collars-leashes/hot-knife/"
"/resources/"
"/"} or

# Those can also be directory browsed with /index.html (or without the trailing
# /). That should be rare. I whitelist it below.
#
# Requests for /files/* might be next most common:

( starts_with(http.request.uri.path, "/files/") and
  substring(http.request.uri.path, -3) in {"pdf" "zip"}
) or

# Only the filetypes pdf & zip exist. Again, /files could be directory browsed
# with /index.html or without trailing /. Whitelisted below.
#
# Next, I enumerate all the individual files acessible in home:

http.request.uri.path in {"/BingSiteAuth.xml" "/llms.txt" "/robots.txt" "/site.webmanifest" "/404.html" "/404.svg" "/sitemap.xml.gz" "/sitemap.xml" "/LICENSE.txt" "/ads.txt" "/6s9dh98hxq1sjut3sf1t1pq5rz41uevv.txt"
"/android-chrome-192x192.png" "/android-chrome-512x512.png" "/apple-touch-icon.png" "/favicon.ico" "/favicon-16x16.png" "/favicon-32x32.png" "/icon.svg"

# I dont serve these files, but redirect them to files I do serve (either as a
# bulk redirect, or a symbolic link):

"/license.txt" "/manifest.webmanifest" "/app-ads.txt" "/security.txt"

# I don't serve these, but see requests for them. I wouldn't block these
# (they're valid). So, I whitelist them (let them get a normal 404 not-found.

"/humans.txt" "/sitemap_index.xml" "/apple-touch-icon-precomposed.png" "/ai.txt" } or

# There's also .well-known. I think that's supposed to be wide-open, any request
# allowed (a standard). But, .php requests seem unreasonable to me. (I'd be
# careful blocking more).

(
starts_with(http.request.uri.path, "/.well-known/") and
  (not ends_with(http.request.uri.path, ".php"))
) or

# Directory browsing shouldn't be allowed for /.well-known. I don't whitelist .index.html
# nor requests without the trailing / . If it's accessed with the trailing /, browsing isn't
# enabled (get the normal not permitted system response).

# At this point, the only other requests that could occur would be directory browsing of
# either 1) directories that aren't the site's "pages" people are invited to visit
# (whitelisted above as directories). Or, 2) directories that were whitelisted above, but
# could be requested with "index.html" added (which would be rare, or withot the trailing
# /).
#
# The following are in the order of what's already been whitelisted (what this adds to, but
# done here because these requests should be very rare.

http.request.uri.path in {

# /directory/ & /directory/index.html

"/images/" "/images/index.html"

# These don't have directory browsing, no .index.html, but shouldn't be blocked.

"/css/"
"/js/"

# These are just /directory/index.html because the /directory/ was already
# whitelisted above:

"/resources/index.html"
"/resources/collars-leashes/index.html"
"/resources/collars-leashes/hot-knife/index.html"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn/index.html"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn/bobbin-winder/index.html"
"/resources/leash-sleeve/index.html"
"/resources/leash-sleeve/template/index.html"
"/index.html"

# Another /directory/ & /directory/index.html

"/files/" "/files/index.html"

# It's also possible to browse the subdirs of /images/, but there's nothing
# to see. I use bulk-redirects to redirect these accesses to /images/. I 
# whitelist the possible ways someone might try to browse these.

"/images/small/" "/images/small/index.html"
"/images/medium/" "/images/medium/index.html"} or

# Finally, the rarest request would be any directory browsing without the trailing slash.
# I think this could only occur if someone typed the url, very rare.

http.request.uri.path in {
"/images"
"/css"
"/js"
"/resources"
"/resources/collars-leashes"
"/resources/collars-leashes/hot-knife"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn/bobbin-winder"
"/resources/leash-sleeve"
"/resources/leash-sleeve/template"
"/files"
"/images/small"
"/images/medium"}

# The closing parenthesis. All the above "or" conditions are one "and" with
# the method and query-string test.

)

# Action: SKIP (all remaining custom rules)
# Log matching requests: NO
#
# Note: It's tempting to consider reversing the logic, and say "if NOT" any of
# the above: block (everthing else). But, I think there could be legitimate
# things browsers, AI bots might look for. For example, llms.txt is relatively
# new. Likewise, an old Apple ipad looks for a "precomposed" favicon. I would
# worry about what else I'm not aware of. I think it's better to let everything
# not-whitelisted fall through. Block malicious things in the next rules.
#
#
# RULE #2: Block: requests for things I don't have (and look malicious):
#
# First, I block anything with the wrong request method (usually POST). And,
# anything with a query string. (Only .css & .js use query strings to bust
# the cache. They were whitelisted.).

(not http.request.method in {"GET" "HEAD" "OPTIONS"}) or
 http.request.uri.query ne "" or

# I block any request for subdirectories (I whitelisted everything valid).

substring(http.request.uri.path, 1) ne remove_bytes(http.request.uri.path, "\x2F") or

# Explanation: that compares the path (starting after the leading "/") to the
# path with all "/" removed. If they don't match, that means the path has a 
# "/" somewhere other than the first byte. It's referencing a subdirectory.
# I whitelisted the valid subdirectories, so... I block it.

# I block semrush & ahrefs. They aren't entirely well-behaved. (If use these 
# free tools to audit my site, I stop blocking these.).

http.user_agent contains "semrush" or
http.user_agent contains "AhrefsBot" or

# Since I blocked subdirectory requests above, the only thing left are requests
# for things in the root directory. I could block anything that wasn't
# whitelisted. But, I worry there could be valid requests for things I don't
# have (not aware of). Blocking specific malicious things is working well:

# If the path has no "." in it, I block it. I'm pretty sure anything valid (that
# I'm not aware of yet) would have a ".".  Anything without .txt, .html, .ico,
# .xml, (etc) would be unusual.

http.request.uri.path eq remove_bytes(http.request.uri.path, "\x2E") or

# Finally, these are the really obviously not-good requests. I start with the 
# end of the path, looking for extensions:

substring(lower(http.request.uri.path),-3) in {".db" ".gz" ".js" ".py" ".ts" ".sh"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path),-4) in {".bak" ".cfg" ".cgi" ".env" ".ini" ".key" ".log" ".mjs" ".old" ".pem" ".php" ".pub" ".sql" ".swp" ".tmp" "vars" ".yml" ".zip"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path),-5) in {".conf" ".json" ".lock" ".php3" ".php6" ".php7" ".toml" ".yaml"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path),-7) in {".js.map" "tfstate"} or

# Then I look for the start of the path:

substring(http.request.uri.path,0,2) in {"/." "/_" "/$" "/;" "/@" "/[" "/*"} or
substring(http.request.uri.path,0,3) in {"/wp"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path),0,4) in {"/db." "/env"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path),0,7) in {"/creden" "/runtim" "/sentry" "/terraf"} or

# A few things I'm not wildcarding yet:

http.request.uri.path in {"application.properties" "/contact.html" "/readme.html"} or

# A few things that could occur anywhere in the path:

lower(http.request.uri.path) contains "config" or
lower(http.request.uri.path) contains "debug" or
http.request.uri.path contains "%" or
http.request.uri.path contains "~" or

# One more thing: If the path is /aa or /bb (or anything where the first 
# and second chars are the same, I block those). I'm not sure about this rule.
# llms.txt would've been blocked (if I hadn't whitelisted it). This might
# apply to more than I want to.

substring(http.request.uri.path,1,2) eq substring(http.request.uri.path,2,3)

#
# ORDER OF PROCESSING
#
# Remember: security "custom rules" are processed before "bulk redirects."
# That's good because it wouldn't make sense to redirect hundreds (thousands) of
# requests only to be blocked. (In the prior section about bulk-redirects,
# I mentioned another place redirects can be done. Those are processed before
# these security rules.).
#
# [1] https://developers.cloudflare.com/ruleset-engine/reference/phases-list/#request-phases
#
